  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA short wave trough will approach from the west early today \nwith rapidly increasing clouds. Increasing forcing for ascent \nwill aid in a shield of overrunning rain moving in from the \nwest beginning this morning. The rain will then spread eastward \nto the rest of the forecast area by early afternoon. There \nremains uncertainty in amounts at this time as dry high pressure\ncentered just to our northeast should continue to advect low \nlevel drier air in from the east. Based on latest CAMs and other\nsources of deterministic and ensemble guidance, have lowered \nprecipitation amounts pretty much across the board. This has \nbeen typical of several rain events this summer, with rainfall \namounts ending up lighter than forecast. Drought will continue \nalthough a few areas across southern zones may see a beneficial \nrainfall.\n\nHourly temperatures may be non-diurnal today as the rain shield\nimpinges on a relatively dry air mass, i.e., temperatures \nshould fall some as the late morning and afternoon progresses \nthrough evaporational cooling.\n\nGradual clearing is expected tonight, especially after midnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEnsemble guidance is in strong agreement that high pressure will \nmove across Canada and arrive in Quebec on Monday. This high to our \nnorth will allow for temperatures to run cooler than normal through \nthe week, with highs in the lower 60s and lows in the upper 30s and \nlower 40s. Frost does appear to be a possibility Tuesday, Wednesday \nand Thursday nights north of the mountains.\n\nAt the end of the week, models show the potential for a coastal low \nto impact the region. A high degree of storm track uncertainty \nremains, but the low could allow for some much needed rainfall over \nthe weekend if the low takes a more westerly track. Gusty winds and \nhigh surf could be possible as well.",
